      "content": "<h3>What is Comparable?</h3>\n<p><strong>Comparable</strong> gives a class its <strong>natural order</strong>: the one default way two objects of that class are compared. It has a single method, <code>compareTo</code>. Once a class implements it, <code>Collections.sort</code>, <code>Arrays.sort</code>, <code>TreeSet</code>, <code>TreeMap</code>, <code>PriorityQueue</code> and <code>stream().sorted()</code> all know how to order it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.lang</code> (no import needed). The interface is <code>Comparable&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li>One method: <code>int compareTo(T other)</code>, implemented <strong>inside the class</strong> being sorted.</li>\n<li>Return <strong>negative</strong> if this object comes first, <strong>zero</strong> if equal in order, <strong>positive</strong> if this comes later.</li>\n<li><code>String</code>, <code>Integer</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Double</code>, <code>Character</code>, <code>LocalDate</code>, <code>BigDecimal</code> and enums are already Comparable.</li>\n<li>There can be <strong>only one</strong> natural order per class. For other orders use <code>Comparator</code>.</li>\n</ul>"
