      "content": "<h3>Rules of a correct compareTo</h3>\n<p>Sorting algorithms assume these rules. Breaking them gives wrong order or <code>IllegalArgumentException: Comparison method violates its general contract</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antisymmetry</strong>: <code>sign(a.compareTo(b)) == -sign(b.compareTo(a))</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Transitivity</strong>: if <code>a &lt; b</code> and <code>b &lt; c</code> then <code>a &lt; c</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Consistency</strong>: if <code>a.compareTo(b) == 0</code> then <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> compare the same way against every other object.</li>\n<li><strong>Consistent with equals</strong> (strongly recommended): <code>a.compareTo(b) == 0</code> exactly when <code>a.equals(b)</code>. Otherwise TreeSet / TreeMap and HashSet / HashMap disagree about duplicates.</li>\n<li><code>compareTo(null)</code> should throw <code>NullPointerException</code>.</li>\n<li>Never compare floating-point with <code>-</code> or by casting; use <code>Double.compare</code> (it also orders NaN and -0.0 correctly).</li>\n</ul>"
